so you're in college right now and you  want to get into data science that's  good  i'm going to talk to you about that and  maybe even give you some helpful tips  i'm richard and this is richard on data  [Music]  so the very first thing that i'm going  to state in this video is that if you're  not in college or you don't have a  degree  you can still break into data science in  fact i have a whole video all about that  but there's no question that degree is  going to help you land that first job as  well as to make more money down the road  not to mention that based on totally  unscientific polling i've done on my own  channel most of my viewers appear to be  in college right now so it's good that  you have your eyes on the prize in this  video i'm going to share with you the  considerations i would carry with you in  order to set yourself up for success as  best as possible  before i do that though i would humbly  ask that you subscribe to my channel if  you haven't already and also take just a  fraction of a second to smash the like  button because that really does help my  content reach a larger audience then if  you want to chip in above and beyond  that to support my channel i'll have  links in the description of this video  to my paypal and patreon accounts as  well as to my crypto wallet addresses  also please note that i'm not a licensed  or professional career advisor or  anything of that nature so please take  this video for some helpful food for  thought as well as for entertainment  purposes  so the very first tip i'm going to give  is going to go for absolutely everybody  it doesn't matter what program or school  you're in it doesn't matter what major  you're in it doesn't even matter if  you're going into data science or not  and that tip is to carry with you a  sense of humility i've seen it happen  many times where somebody gets a degree  from a top-notch university  and then they somehow think that this  entitles them to things or that it's  automatically a guarantee of ongoing  success  if that's you you may very well get  thrown into real world problems and get  smacked in the face  because it's often when you get out of  college and you start solving real  problems for real businesses that you  learn exactly how much you don't  actually know yet  learning really is a forever process and  college is a great first step at that  but it really is just a first step and  you don't get a shortcut or a pass on  the rest of the process of learning just  because you have a really good degree  now that we've got that one out of the  way let's actually talk about leveraging  your college or university to acquire  the necessary skill sets that being said  i would be lying to you if i told you  there weren't companies that will  automatically exclude you if you don't  have a particular major  and the most common types of majors that  fit into this umbrella are statistics  computer science information technology  pure data science  maybe economics and business but then  what are the actual skills you need to  develop for data science  first of all you absolutely need to have  some exposure to statistics this is  going to be your foundation and now i  did a video all about how much  statistics you need and the link will be  in the description to that but the  reason i call it the foundation is  because it gives you different methods  for attacking problems and it helps you  reason through problems it helps you  understand all the underlying  considerations with real world problems  and it prevents you from delving into  pseudoscience it keeps what you're doing  actually scientific then you should also  ideally get some course work in on  databases and multi-relational data  structures  in particular something that's going to  cover sql you don't need to go crazy  with this necessarily but if you can  learn sql in the course of your college  education it's really going to pay  dividends for you some computer science  is also going to serve you really well  so at least from my experience usually  when you learn one programming language  it makes it significantly easier to  learn the next one  so don't stress out too much over the  specific language that you're learning  but if you get some exposure to python  that's that's even more awesome and  while you're at it if you get some  exposure to microsoft azure or aws  that's going to be really helpful too  now the next tip i'm going to give at  this point is to be a little bit  cautious with professors it's been my  experience anyway that there are  professors out there who are amazing but  there are also those who've been stuck  in academia for 30 plus years they've  gotten themselves into a bit of a bubble  and then they design their courses like  academic exercises those are usually the  types of courses that you end up using  and remembering nothing from it might be  help to talk to departmental counselors  or other people that you trust to figure  out which professors came from recent  industrial experience  even if those are actually lecturers who  probably get paid a lot less than full  tenure tracked professors  there's a good chance you'll learn a lot  more from those courses and that they'll  be a lot more practical yes i understand  this is not something always that you  have a lot of flexibility with for  instance sometimes your program will  require certain courses you don't get to  choose your professor but when you're  picking things like electives it's  certainly something to think about  keeping up with the theme of doing true  practical things you want to get the  chance to do some real project work i  know everybody hates group projects but  these really are some of the absolute  best learning experiences that you can  get for real-world data science when  you're in college don't be that person  who sits back never shows up to meetings  and lets your whole team do all the work  you're shortchanging yourself on a lot  of valuable learning experiences and  plus your whole team is going to hate  you you want to be actually really  trying in these projects and then at the  end saving and holding on to your final  work keep a jupiter notebook or r  markdown file or at least a word  document or something  then document the problem that you were  working on the code the solution you  came up with and then interpret that in  real world terms  even if you're somebody looking for a  job with zero work experience up to this  point even being able to do something  like this is going to give you a leg up  over other people and i guarantee you  it's going to impress people that you  talk with a lot more than some final  grade that you get in some arbitrary  class  if you literally don't get any chances  whatsoever to do projects like this your  next best bet would probably be to go to  kaggle and do some challenges there but  you want to at least have something  tangible that you can talk about on your  resume or on your linkedin profile  even better yet a little portfolio with  code that you've written that can go  somewhere like github now as far as  actually looking for a job is concerned  you want to be looking at the market as  soon as possible the first reason for  that is you want to get an internship if  you can  there's plenty of companies out there  that love to hire interns they're way  cheaper than regular full-time employees  and they're really helpful for just  getting stuff off the company's plate  and frankly there is a pretty well-known  catch-22 situation with a lot of  companies out there that is you need to  have experience to get a job but you  can't get a job sometimes because you  don't have experience first if you're  able to get around that problem by let's  say in your sophomore or junior year or  certainly if you're in grad school  getting an internship during the summer  that's going to pay off massively for  you  especially if you get a chance at that  internship to actually write code and  just keep building that portfolio out  so now the last major thing i would say  here is to really figure out how much  college you actually need just speaking  personally on this one when i was part  way through my bachelor's degree i was  looking at jobs in my local area and i  found out that a huge number of them  either outright required a master's  degree or at least stated that it was a  strong preference of theirs  sadly that has become a recent trend  where master's degrees are highly  preferred for a lot of jobs  but that is subject to change based on  macroeconomic factors and there are tons  of exceptions to that rule google being  a pretty obvious example where they  overtly care nothing about your degree  and personally this is just me but i  didn't want to be a broke college  student then go out and make money then  leave that job only to go back and live  like a broke college student again  i wanted to get all that school out of  the way so that i'd never have to go  back to it again but ultimately that's a  completely personal decision that only  you can make but you still want to make  the most informed possible decision that  you can and that's where getting a feel  for the market is going to help you  whether that's through sites like  linkedin or indeed and increasingly in  this post-pandemic era you may also be  able to find remote jobs in other states  or locations  so that's probably something you want to  consider above and beyond your own local  area if you're okay with working in a  remote capacity if you see a lot of  positions out there that require just a  bachelor's there's absolutely no reason  to feel obligated or forced to get a  master's or especially a phd i should  tell you in full transparency that those  advanced degrees are going to increase  your likelihood of finding jobs and  netting a higher salary but they  absolutely are not required it's all  just a game of probabilities so those  are all of my tips for breaking into  data science for college students  or i should say a combination of things  i personally did that worked as well as  some other things that i wish i had done  if you're going to go to college in the  first place do it right  don't be one of those people who gets  out with nothing to show for it but a  pile of student loan debt regrets and  maybe even some liver damage  sure have some fun while you're at it i  know i did but if you're interested in  data science and you're already paying  for college that means you're probably  surrounded with learning opportunities  all around you whether you realize them  or not the final gpa that you have at  the end probably doesn't matter nearly  as much as you think in fact the name of  the college or university that you're at  also probably isn't going to matter much  in the long run  but the learning opportunities that you  get assuming you truly learn the  necessary material  that is going to carry with you into the  future probably more so than you can  even imagine  so thanks for watching this video if you  enjoyed it smash the like button it only  takes about a half a second  and then leave me a comment down below  let me know what you think  did you agree with my points here  anything that you disagreed with let me  know  then i'll see you all in the not so  distant future  until then  richard  on data

